/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/sc-200.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("sc-200", [["sa1",0,1,"A legitimate admin tool on one server keeps raising the same Defender for Endpoint alert. The SOC wants to stop that alert only for that file on that server and keep all other protection. What should they create?",["An allow indicator for the file hash across the tenant","A device group with automation set to no response","Offboarding the server from Defender for Endpoint","An alert tuning rule scoped to that file and device"],3,"Alert tuning (suppression) rules hide or auto-resolve a specific benign alert pattern and leave the sensor running. An allow indicator is tempting, but it changes prevention for every device in the tenant, not just alerting on one server.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure settings in Microsoft Defender XDR",["An allow indicator changes prevention for that hash on every device in the tenant, which is far broader than silencing one alert on one server.","A device group with no automated response only changes how automated investigations remediate; the alert would still fire.","Offboarding removes all Defender for Endpoint protection and visibility from the server, which breaks the requirement to keep other protection.",null],null],["sa2",0,1,"The SOC manager wants an email every time Defender XDR creates a high-severity incident. What should be configured?",["The Security Operator role for the manager's account","An incident notification rule filtered to high severity","A Sentinel watchlist that holds the manager's email address","The unified audit log setting in Microsoft Purview"],1,"Incident notification rules in the Defender portal settings send email to chosen recipients for incidents that match filters such as severity. A role only grants portal access; it does not send email.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure settings in Microsoft Defender XDR",["The Security Operator role only grants permissions in the portal; roles never send email notifications.",null,"A watchlist is reference data queried in KQL inside Sentinel; storing an email address there does not send anything.","The unified audit log records user and admin activity for searches; it is not a notification mechanism for incidents."],null],["sa3",0,1,"Before enforcing the attack surface reduction rule that blocks credential theft from LSASS, the endpoint team wants to see which apps it would affect. What should they do first?",["Deploy the rule in audit mode and review the events it logs","Deploy the rule in block mode on the domain controllers","Turn on controlled folder access in block mode instead","Create a custom detection rule that copies the rule's ASR logic"],0,"Audit mode logs what the ASR rule would have blocked without breaking anything, so you can add exclusions before enforcing. Block mode on critical servers first is the risky choice this is meant to avoid.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure protections",[null,"Block mode enforces the rule immediately and could break legitimate apps on critical servers, which is exactly what the team wants to find out first.","Controlled folder access protects folders from ransomware writes; it is a different feature and tells you nothing about the LSASS rule's impact.","A custom detection only raises alerts from hunting data and cannot reproduce or preview the ASR engine's blocking behavior."],null],["sa4",0,1,"An attacker with local admin rights tries to turn off Microsoft Defender Antivirus real-time protection through the registry. Which feature prevents this change?",["Network protection","Controlled folder access","Tamper protection","Device discovery"],2,"Tamper protection locks security settings such as real-time protection so they cannot be changed locally, even by admins. Network protection blocks connections to bad domains; it does not protect settings.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure protections",["Network protection blocks outbound connections to malicious domains and IPs; it does not lock antivirus settings.","Controlled folder access stops untrusted apps writing to protected folders, not changes to Defender's own configuration.",null,"Device discovery finds unmanaged devices on the network; it has no role in protecting security settings."],null],["sa5",0,1,"Tier-1 analysts in the EU office should see and act on EU endpoints only in Defender for Endpoint. What should the administrator do?",["Create an EU device group and give only the analysts' group access to it","Tag the EU devices and give the analysts the Security Reader role in Entra","Build a separate Log Analytics workspace for EU devices","Add a Conditional Access policy that blocks non-EU pages"],0,"With Defender XDR RBAC, device groups limit which devices a user group can see and manage. An Entra role such as Security Reader applies to the whole tenant, so tags alone do not restrict it.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",[null,"Security Reader is a tenant-wide Entra role, so tags alone would not limit the analysts to EU devices, and Reader cannot take response actions.","Defender for Endpoint data is not scoped by Log Analytics workspaces; a separate workspace would not restrict the Defender portal.","Conditional Access controls sign-in conditions to apps, not which devices a user can see inside the Defender portal."],null],["sa6",0,1,"A company runs workloads on Azure VMs and on AWS EC2 and wants Defender for Cloud server threat detection on both. What should be done?",["Install the Defender for Identity sensor on each EC2 instance","Connect the AWS account to Defender for Cloud and enable Defender for Servers","Rely on foundational CSPM, which adds server threat alerts","Forward EC2 syslog to Sentinel through the Syslog connector"],1,"Defender for Cloud uses a multicloud connector for AWS, and the Defender for Servers plan adds workload threat protection, including Defender for Endpoint. Foundational CSPM only gives posture recommendations and secure score, not threat alerts.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",["The Defender for Identity sensor is for domain controllers and identity servers such as AD FS and AD CS, not for EC2 workload protection.",null,"Foundational CSPM gives free posture recommendations and secure score only; it does not add server threat detection.","Forwarding syslog to Sentinel gives raw logs for your own rules but does not enable Defender for Cloud's server threat protection."],null],["sa7",0,1,"Which Defender for Cloud capability gives secure score and basic security recommendations at no extra charge?",["Defender CSPM","Defender for Servers Plan 2","Foundational CSPM","Defender for Storage"],2,"Foundational CSPM is on by default and free. Defender CSPM adds paid features such as attack path analysis, and the Defender for plans are paid workload protections.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",["Defender CSPM is the paid posture plan that adds features such as attack path analysis and the cloud security explorer.","Defender for Servers Plan 2 is a paid workload protection plan for machines, not the free secure score baseline.",null,"Defender for Storage is a paid plan that detects threats against storage accounts, not the free posture baseline."],null],["sa8",0,1,"An analyst must be able to assign, comment on and close Sentinel incidents but must not create or edit analytics rules. Which built-in role fits?",["Microsoft Sentinel Reader","Microsoft Sentinel Contributor","Log Analytics Reader","Microsoft Sentinel Responder"],3,"Responder can manage incidents (assign, change status, close) but cannot create or change content. Contributor can also edit rules, which is more than needed, and Reader cannot change incidents.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",["Sentinel Reader can view incidents and content but cannot assign, change or close incidents.","Sentinel Contributor can also create and edit analytics rules and other content, which exceeds what this analyst should have.","Log Analytics Reader only allows reading workspace data and gives no rights to manage Sentinel incidents.",null],null],["sa9",0,1,"An automation rule should run a playbook, but the playbook's resource group appears greyed out when you pick it. What is the most likely fix?",["Move the playbook into the Log Analytics workspace itself","Grant Sentinel the Automation Contributor role on that resource group","Give the analyst the Logic App Contributor role on the whole subscription","Re-create the playbook with an alert trigger rather than incident"],1,"Sentinel needs explicit permission (Microsoft Sentinel Automation Contributor) on the playbook's resource group before automation rules can run playbooks there. The analyst's own Logic App rights do not give Sentinel's service account that permission.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure automation",["Playbooks are Logic Apps that live in a resource group; they cannot be moved inside a Log Analytics workspace.",null,"The analyst's own Logic App rights do not give the Sentinel service account permission to run playbooks in that resource group.","The trigger type affects which playbooks can be run, but the greyed-out resource group is a permissions problem, not a trigger problem."],null],["sa10",0,1,"A firewall sends logs in Common Event Format. How should they reach Sentinel today?",["Through a Linux forwarder using the CEF via AMA connector and a DCR","Through the legacy Log Analytics agent installed directly on the firewall","Through the Windows Security Events via AMA connector","Straight from the firewall to the workspace with no forwarder"],0,"Appliances that can't run an agent send CEF over syslog to a Linux forwarder running the Azure Monitor Agent, and a data collection rule sends the events to the CommonSecurityLog table. The legacy Log Analytics agent (MMA) is retired.","Manage a security operations environment: Ingest data sources in Microsoft Sentinel",[null,"The legacy Log Analytics agent (MMA) is retired, and firewalls generally cannot run an agent at all.","Windows Security Events via AMA collects Windows event logs from Windows machines, not CEF over syslog from appliances.","Firewalls cannot write directly to a Log Analytics workspace; CEF needs a forwarder with the Azure Monitor Agent and a DCR."],null],["sa11",0,1,"Ingestion costs from domain controllers are too high. The SOC only needs a small set of specific Windows event IDs. What is the best approach?",["The All Security Events set, then filtering later inside analytics rules","A watchlist of the needed event IDs linked to the connector","A data collection rule with a custom XPath filter for those event IDs","Workspace retention lowered to 30 days to offset ingestion"],2,"DCRs filter events at the source, so you pay only for what you collect. Filtering inside analytics rules still ingests and bills every event, and lowering retention does not reduce ingestion charges.","Manage a security operations environment: Ingest data sources in Microsoft Sentinel",["Collecting All Security Events ingests and bills every event; filtering later in rules does not reduce ingestion cost.","Watchlists are reference data for queries and cannot be linked to a connector to filter what gets collected.",null,"Retention is billed separately from ingestion, so shortening retention does not cut the ingestion charges that are the problem."],null],["sa12",0,1,"A team wants the data connector, analytics rules, workbooks and hunting queries for a third-party product installed together. Where should they go?",["The Community tab of the Sentinel GitHub repository only","The Analytics rule templates page, one template at a time","The Data connectors page of the Azure Monitor portal","The product's solution in the Sentinel Content hub"],3,"Content hub solutions package connectors, rules, workbooks, parsers and hunting queries so they can be installed and updated as one unit. Adding templates one by one misses the connector and workbook.","Manage a security operations environment: Ingest data sources in Microsoft Sentinel",["The GitHub repository hosts community content, but installing from it manually does not give a packaged, updatable solution with its connector.","Adding rule templates one at a time misses the connector, workbooks and hunting queries that ship with the product.","Azure Monitor's own pages manage agents and DCRs; they do not install Sentinel content packages.",null],null],["sa13",0,1,"Developers want an in-house application to send JSON security events to a custom Sentinel table over HTTPS. Which method should they use?",["The HTTP Data Collector API with a shared workspace key","The Logs Ingestion API with a data collection rule and a custom table","A Syslog via AMA connector on the application server","Windows Event Forwarding to a collector server"],1,"The Logs Ingestion API sends data through a DCR, which can transform it, into a custom table using Entra ID authentication. The older HTTP Data Collector API is being retired and relies on a shared key.","Manage a security operations environment: Ingest data sources in Microsoft Sentinel",["The HTTP Data Collector API is the legacy method that uses a shared workspace key and is being retired.",null,"Syslog via AMA collects syslog messages from Linux hosts; it is not an HTTPS API for an application to post JSON.","Windows Event Forwarding moves Windows event logs between servers and does not accept custom JSON from an application."],null],["sa14",0,1,"Why should a scheduled analytics rule map entities such as Account, Host and IP?",["So the query runs faster against the analytics tier","So the rule can run as a near-real-time rule","So alerts carry structured entities for pivoting, grouping and UEBA","So Sentinel can skip ingesting unmapped columns"],2,"Entity mapping turns query columns into entities that drive the investigation graph, entity pages, alert grouping and correlation. It does not change query speed or what is ingested.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["Entity mapping happens after the query returns results; it does not make the query run any faster.","NRT is a separate rule type chosen when the rule is created; entity mapping does not change the rule type.",null,"Entity mapping has no effect on ingestion; every column in the table is still stored whether it is mapped or not."],null],["sa15",0,1,"A detection for adding a user to Domain Admins must raise an alert within about a minute of the event arriving. Which rule type fits best?",["A near-real-time (NRT) analytics rule","A scheduled rule set to run every five minutes","An anomaly rule based on machine learning","A Fusion multistage attack rule"],0,"NRT rules run about once a minute on newly arrived data, which suits simple, high-value events. Scheduled rules can't run more often than every five minutes, and anomaly and Fusion rules aren't built for a specific single event.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",[null,"Scheduled rules run at most every five minutes and usually add ingestion delay, so they cannot reliably alert within about a minute.","Anomaly rules use machine learning baselines over time and are not designed to fire on a single specific event.","Fusion correlates multiple alerts into multistage incidents; it is not a rule you write to catch one specific event quickly."],null],["sa16",0,1,"A brute-force rule creates hundreds of incidents a day, one for every alert about the same user. What reduces this without losing alerts?",["A higher rule threshold, so that most of the alerts no longer fire","An automation rule that closes every new incident","A switch from a scheduled rule to an NRT rule","Alert grouping by matching entities within a time window"],3,"Alert grouping adds related alerts (for example, the same account within 24 hours) to one incident, so nothing is lost. Raising the threshold or auto-closing incidents hides real detections.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["Raising the threshold suppresses real detections instead of consolidating them, so alerts are lost.","Automatically closing every incident hides genuine attacks and defeats the purpose of the rule.","Switching to NRT changes how often the rule runs but does nothing to group related alerts into one incident.",null],null],["sa17",0,1,"After turning on the Microsoft Defender XDR connector with incident integration in Sentinel, the team sees duplicate incidents for the same Defender alerts. What should they do?",["Delete the Defender XDR connector and use the older product connectors","Turn off Microsoft incident creation rules for the Defender products","Add an automation rule that closes incidents from Defender","Lower the severity of all Defender alerts in Sentinel"],1,"With incident integration, Defender XDR incidents sync into Sentinel. Microsoft incident creation rules for the same products create a second copy, so they should be turned off (the connector offers to do this).","Manage a security operations environment: Ingest data sources in Microsoft Sentinel",["The Defender XDR connector with incident integration is the recommended approach; going back to older connectors loses correlation and sync.",null,"Auto-closing Defender incidents hides the synced copies too and treats the symptom rather than removing the duplicate source.","Changing severity does not stop two incidents from being created for the same alerts."],null],["sa18",0,1,"Which columns must a Defender XDR advanced hunting query return before it can be saved as a custom detection rule?",["Only Timestamp and a Severity value calculated in the query","A column named AlertTitle and one named MitreTechnique","Timestamp, an event ID such as ReportId, and an entity column such as DeviceId","Any columns, as long as the query uses summarize"],2,"Custom detections need the event time, a column that identifies the event record and at least one impacted-entity column so alerts can be linked to devices, users or mailboxes. Severity and MITRE techniques are set in the rule wizard, not in query columns.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["Severity is chosen in the rule wizard, and Timestamp alone is not enough; the query also needs a record ID and an entity column.","AlertTitle and MITRE techniques are entered in the wizard's alert details, not returned as query columns.",null,"summarize is not required, and a query that summarizes away the required columns cannot be saved as a detection."],null],["sa19",0,1,"Incidents from one specific analytics rule should be assigned to the Tier-2 queue and have their severity raised automatically. What is the simplest solution?",["An automation rule that runs on incident creation","A playbook with an alert trigger that calls the Graph API","A change to the rule's KQL that adds an Owner column","A workbook that highlights incidents from that rule"],0,"Automation rules can set the owner, severity, status, tags and tasks with no code. A playbook could do it too, but it adds cost and complexity for something built in.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure automation",[null,"A playbook could do this but adds a Logic App, cost and code for something automation rules do natively.","Adding an Owner column to the query output does not assign the incident to anyone in Sentinel.","A workbook only visualizes data; it cannot change an incident's owner or severity."],null],["sa20",0,1,"When a new incident is created, the SOC wants to look up each IP on a reputation service and post the results to a Teams channel. What should be built?",["An automation rule on its own, using a built-in 'post message' condition","A watchlist refreshed from the reputation service","A scheduled analytics rule that queries the reputation site","A playbook run by an automation rule when an incident is created"],3,"Calling external APIs and posting to Teams needs Logic Apps connectors, which is what playbooks provide. The automation rule is the trigger that runs the playbook.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure automation",["Automation rules have no built-in action to call external APIs or post to Teams; they can only run a playbook for that.","A watchlist stores reference data and cannot post enrichment results to Teams when an incident is created.","An analytics rule queries data in the workspace; it cannot call an external reputation website or post messages.",null],null],["sa21",0,1,"Analysts need a list of VIP accounts that several analytics rules can reference and that HR can update from a CSV. What should be used?",["A custom table filled through the Logs Ingestion API","A hard-coded dynamic() list inside each rule","A Sentinel watchlist queried with _GetWatchlist()","A Defender XDR device tag named VIP"],2,"Watchlists hold reference data, are easy to update from CSV, and are read in KQL with _GetWatchlist. Hard-coding the list in each rule means editing every rule whenever it changes.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["A custom table via the Logs Ingestion API is heavier to build and maintain, and HR could not simply upload a CSV to update it.","Hard-coding a dynamic() list in each rule means editing every rule whenever the VIP list changes.",null,"Device tags label devices in Defender XDR; they cannot hold a list of user accounts for Sentinel rules to reference."],null],["sa22",0,1,"The SOC lead wants to see which MITRE ATT&CK techniques are covered by active analytics rules and which have no coverage. Where should they look?",["The Threat analytics page in Defender XDR","The MITRE ATT&CK page in Microsoft Sentinel","The Secure score page in Defender for Cloud","The Content hub filtered by vendor"],1,"Sentinel's MITRE ATT&CK view shows coverage by active and available rules for each technique. Threat analytics covers threat campaigns, not your rule coverage.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["Threat analytics covers Microsoft's reports on threat campaigns and your exposure, not which techniques your own rules cover.",null,"Secure score in Defender for Cloud measures configuration posture, not MITRE ATT&CK detection coverage.","Filtering Content hub by vendor shows installable solutions, not a coverage map of your active rules by technique."],null],["sa23",0,1,"Which Sentinel feature recommends actions such as cutting ingestion of data no rule uses or adding detections for gaps against common attack scenarios?",["UEBA","Workbooks","Livestream","SOC optimization"],3,"SOC optimization analyzes your ingestion and detections and suggests cost and coverage improvements. Workbooks only show whatever data you query.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",["UEBA builds behavioral baselines for users and entities; it does not recommend ingestion or detection changes.","Workbooks display whatever queries you build; they do not analyze your SOC and suggest improvements.","Livestream runs a hunting query continuously against new data; it makes no cost or coverage recommendations.",null],null],["sa24",0,1,"High-volume network flow logs must be kept for a year for occasional investigations, but no analytics rule needs them. What is the most cost-effective option?",["Store them in the Sentinel data lake tier and query them when needed","Keep them in the analytics tier with one year of interactive retention","Load them into a watchlist so analysts can search them","Ingest them daily, then delete them after 30 days"],0,"The data lake tier is built for cheap long-term storage of high-volume data queried now and then. The analytics tier is the expensive choice here, since no detection needs the data.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",[null,"A year of interactive analytics retention for high-volume logs is the most expensive option, and no detection needs that speed.","Watchlists are small reference lists with size limits, not a store for high-volume flow logs.","Deleting the logs after 30 days fails the requirement to keep them for a year."],null],["sa25",0,1,"Which Sentinel feature builds interactive dashboards of ingestion volume, incident trends and SOC metrics from KQL queries?",["Workbooks","Watchlists","Notebooks","Automation rules"],0,"Workbooks are Azure Monitor dashboards driven by KQL. Notebooks are Jupyter environments for deeper analysis, not dashboards.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",[null,"Watchlists hold reference data such as VIP lists; they do not render dashboards.","Notebooks are Jupyter environments for deep analysis with Python, not interactive KQL dashboards for SOC metrics.","Automation rules act on incidents (assign, tag, run playbooks); they produce no visualizations."],null],["sa26",0,1,"The data team wants alerts on SQL injection attempts against an Azure SQL database. Which plan should be turned on?",["Defender for Storage","Defender for Databases (Defender for Azure SQL)","Defender CSPM","Defender for App Service"],1,"The database plan detects threats such as SQL injection and unusual access to Azure SQL. Defender for Storage watches storage accounts, not SQL queries.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",["Defender for Storage detects threats against storage accounts, such as malware uploads, not SQL injection.",null,"Defender CSPM adds posture features like attack path analysis; it does not raise SQL injection threat alerts.","Defender for App Service protects web apps hosted in App Service, not the Azure SQL database itself."],null],["sa27",0,1,"Where must the Defender for Identity sensor be installed to monitor on-premises Active Directory authentication?",["On every member workstation in the domain","On the Microsoft Entra Connect server only, not the DCs","On domain controllers and AD FS or AD CS servers","On a Linux syslog forwarder in the DMZ"],2,"The sensor reads authentication traffic and events directly on domain controllers (and identity servers such as AD FS and AD CS). Putting it only on the Entra Connect server misses DC traffic.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",["Workstations are covered by Defender for Endpoint; the Defender for Identity sensor belongs on identity infrastructure.","The Entra Connect server can have a sensor, but installing only there misses the authentication traffic on domain controllers.",null,"The sensor runs on Windows identity servers and reads traffic locally; a Linux syslog forwarder is not a supported placement."],null],["sa28",0,1,"Users sometimes click links in email that were safe at delivery but later start hosting a phishing page. Which Defender for Office 365 feature protects them?",["Safe Attachments dynamic delivery","Zero-hour auto purge for malware only","Anti-spam outbound policy","Safe Links time-of-click URL checks"],3,"Safe Links rewrites URLs and checks them again when the user clicks, so it catches links that turn malicious after delivery. Safe Attachments detonates attachments, not links.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure protections",["Safe Attachments detonates attachments in a sandbox; dynamic delivery handles attachments, not links in the message body.","Zero-hour auto purge can remove messages after delivery, but limiting it to malware does not protect a user clicking a link that turned malicious.","Outbound anti-spam policies control mail your users send, not inbound links they click.",null],null],["sa29",0,1,"What is the easiest way to onboard hundreds of Azure Windows and Linux VMs to Defender for Endpoint?",["Run the local onboarding script on each VM by hand","Install the Defender for Identity sensor on each VM","Enable Defender for Servers, which deploys the Defender for Endpoint integration","Connect the VMs through the Windows Security Events connector"],2,"Defender for Servers includes Defender for Endpoint and can deploy the sensor to supported machines automatically. Local scripts work but don't scale.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",["Running the local script on each VM works but does not scale to hundreds of machines and is error-prone.","The Defender for Identity sensor monitors Active Directory on DCs; it does not onboard VMs to Defender for Endpoint.",null,"The Windows Security Events connector sends event logs to Sentinel; it does not onboard machines to Defender for Endpoint and ignores Linux."],null],["sa30",0,1,"The SOC suspects there are unmanaged laptops and IoT devices on the corporate network. Which Defender for Endpoint feature can find them using onboarded devices?",["Tamper protection","Live response","Attack surface reduction","Device discovery"],3,"Device discovery uses onboarded endpoints to find unmanaged devices on the same networks, so they can be onboarded or assessed. Live response is a remote shell on devices you already manage.","Manage a security operations environment: Manage assets and environments",["Tamper protection locks Defender settings on managed devices; it does not find unmanaged ones.","Live response is a remote shell on devices that are already onboarded, not a discovery tool.","Attack surface reduction rules block risky behaviors on managed endpoints and do not inventory unknown devices.",null],null],["sa31",0,1,"The SOC wants to block all endpoints from reaching a known command-and-control domain. What must be in place for a domain indicator with a Block action to work?",["Tamper protection turned off","Network protection turned on in block mode","Controlled folder access turned on in audit mode","A Sentinel TI data connector"],1,"URL and domain indicators are enforced through network protection (and SmartScreen in Edge). Without network protection, other browsers and processes aren't blocked.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure protections",["Turning tamper protection off weakens security and has nothing to do with enforcing domain indicators.",null,"Controlled folder access protects folders from untrusted writes; audit mode would not block anything anyway.","A Sentinel TI connector brings indicators into Sentinel for matching, but it does not enforce blocking on endpoints."],null],["sa32",0,1,"Analysts want to work Sentinel incidents and Defender XDR incidents in one queue in the Defender portal. What is required?",["Onboard the Sentinel workspace to the Defender portal","Export Sentinel incidents into a Defender watchlist","Assign every analyst the Global Administrator role","Build a Logic App that copies incidents between portals"],0,"Connecting the workspace to the Defender portal (the unified security operations platform) puts Sentinel and Defender XDR incidents in one queue with shared hunting. Global Administrator is too broad and doesn't connect anything.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",[null,"Watchlists hold reference data; exporting incidents into one does not create a shared incident queue.","Global Administrator is excessive privilege and does not connect Sentinel to the Defender portal.","A custom Logic App copying incidents is fragile and unnecessary when the workspace can be onboarded to the unified platform."],null],["sa33",0,1,"Analysts want each user's sign-ins compared with that user's normal behavior and with peers, shown as insights on entity pages. What must be turned on?",["The Fusion rule with all alert sources","User and Entity Behavior Analytics (UEBA) with its data sources","A workbook built on the SigninLogs table","Defender for Cloud Apps session policies"],1,"UEBA builds baselines from sources such as sign-in and audit logs and adds anomalies and peer comparisons to entity pages. Fusion correlates alerts; it doesn't build per-user baselines.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",["Fusion correlates alerts into multistage incidents; it does not build per-user baselines or peer comparisons.",null,"A workbook on SigninLogs can chart sign-ins but does not calculate behavioral baselines or add insights to entity pages.","Session policies in Defender for Cloud Apps control live sessions to apps; they do not add UEBA insights in Sentinel."],null],["sa34",0,1,"A partner shares threat indicators through a STIX/TAXII 2.x server. How should Sentinel ingest them?",["The Syslog via AMA connector","A weekly CSV upload into a Sentinel watchlist","The Defender for Cloud Apps connector","The Threat Intelligence TAXII data connector"],3,"The TAXII connector polls STIX feeds and stores the indicators as threat intelligence objects that rules and hunting can match. A weekly watchlist upload goes stale and loses the STIX details.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["Syslog via AMA collects syslog messages from Linux hosts; it cannot poll a STIX/TAXII server.","A weekly CSV watchlist upload is manual, goes stale and discards the STIX object details.","The Defender for Cloud Apps connector brings in cloud app alerts and logs, not partner threat intelligence feeds.",null],null],["sa35",0,1,"Verbose proxy logs sit in a low-cost tier. The SOC wants an hourly aggregate of bytes per user in the analytics tier for dashboards and detections. What should they create?",["A near-real-time rule on the low-cost tier","A watchlist refreshed every hour","A summary rule that writes aggregated results to an analytics table","A workbook with auto-refresh turned on"],2,"Summary rules run a KQL aggregation on a schedule and save the smaller result in an analytics-tier table, which cuts cost while keeping the data useful. A workbook only displays data; it doesn't store results for rules.","Manage a security operations environment: Design and configure a Microsoft Sentinel workspace",["NRT rules run against analytics-tier data every minute and do not write aggregated results to a table.","A watchlist is manually maintained reference data, not a scheduled aggregation of proxy logs.",null,"Auto-refresh only redraws a dashboard; it does not store results that analytics rules can query."],null],["sa36",0,1,"Which Sentinel analytics rule type correlates low-fidelity alerts and anomalies from several products into high-fidelity multistage attack incidents?",["Fusion","Near-real-time (NRT)","Microsoft incident creation","Anomaly"],0,"Fusion uses machine learning to correlate signals across products into multistage incidents and is on by default. Anomaly rules produce anomaly records on their own and are one of the inputs Fusion can use.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",[null,"NRT rules run a single KQL query about every minute; they do not correlate alerts across products.","Microsoft incident creation rules turn alerts from Microsoft products into incidents one for one, without multistage correlation.","Anomaly rules produce individual anomaly records; they are an input Fusion can use, not the correlation engine itself."],null],["sa37",0,1,"Analysts want the suspicious command line shown on each alert from a scheduled rule so they can filter and triage without opening raw logs. What should the rule author configure?",["A File entity mapped to the CommandLine column value","Custom details that surface the CommandLine column","A watchlist containing the command lines seen so far","Alert grouping that groups incidents by the command line"],1,"Custom details copy chosen query columns into the alert so they appear on the alert and incident. A File entity describes a file, not a command line, so mapping the command line to it is misleading.","Manage a security operations environment: Configure detections",["A File entity describes a file, and forcing a command line into it produces misleading entities rather than a readable field.",null,"A watchlist of past command lines does not put the current alert's command line on the alert.","Alert grouping controls how alerts form incidents; it does not show the command line on each alert."],null],["sa38",0,2,"A Defender XDR incident links alerts from email, a laptop and an identity. The analyst wants the sequence of events and assets on one page. Where should they look?",["The device's software inventory","The Secure score page","The incident's attack story","The Action center history tab"],2,"The attack story shows correlated alerts in order with an incident graph of the affected assets. The software inventory lists installed programs, not the attack sequence.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",["Software inventory lists installed programs on one device, not the correlated sequence of events across assets.","Secure score measures security posture; it contains no incident timeline.",null,"The Action center history shows remediation actions that ran, not the full attack sequence and affected assets."],null],["sa39",0,2,"A laptop is beaconing to a command-and-control server. The analyst must stop lateral movement but keep collecting telemetry and running response actions. Which action fits?",["Isolate the device","Restrict app execution","Offboard the device","Run a quick antivirus scan"],0,"Isolation blocks all network traffic except the connection to the Defender service, so investigation and response can continue. Offboarding would remove your visibility.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",[null,"Restrict app execution blocks unsigned code but leaves network connectivity up, so lateral movement over the network is still possible.","Offboarding removes the sensor, so you lose the telemetry and response actions you need to keep.","An antivirus scan may find malware but does nothing to stop the device communicating with other hosts."],null],["sa40",0,2,"The incident responder needs autoruns, running processes, network connections and event logs from a remote device in one download. Which action should they use?",["Run antivirus scan","Initiate automated investigation","Restrict app execution","Collect investigation package"],3,"The investigation package gathers forensic data such as processes, services, scheduled tasks, network connections and logs into a zip file. A scan only looks for malware.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",["An antivirus scan looks for malware and returns detections, not a forensic bundle of processes, autoruns and logs.","An automated investigation analyzes and remediates on its own; it does not give the responder a downloadable forensic package.","Restrict app execution limits which code can run; it collects no data.",null],null],["sa41",0,2,"An analyst needs to pull a suspicious DLL from a remote endpoint for analysis and list its scheduled tasks. Which capability allows this?",["A live response session with commands such as getfile","The file page's 'Download file' button, which works in all cases","Threat Explorer with the file hash","An advanced hunting query on DeviceFileEvents"],0,"Live response gives a remote shell for collecting files, running approved scripts and checking state. Advanced hunting shows events about the file but can't retrieve it.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",[null,"Download file on the file page depends on the file having been collected and is not always available; it also cannot list scheduled tasks.","Threat Explorer is a Defender for Office 365 tool for email; it cannot retrieve files from an endpoint.","Advanced hunting shows events about the file but cannot pull the file itself or run commands on the device."],null],["sa42",0,2,"A malicious executable is running on several devices. The analyst wants to kill its processes and quarantine it wherever it is found. Which action should they take from the file page?",["Add the file to an alert tuning rule","Stop and quarantine file","Collect investigation package on each device","Submit the file for deep analysis only"],1,"Stop and quarantine file ends running processes and quarantines the file on devices where it is found. Deep analysis gives information but doesn't contain anything.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",["Alert tuning only hides or resolves alerts; the malware would keep running.",null,"Collecting investigation packages gathers forensic data but does not kill processes or quarantine the file.","Deep analysis detonates the file for a report; it contains nothing on the affected devices."],null],["sa43",0,2,"A finance user's laptop runs an unsigned hacking tool, but the user must keep working on email and Teams during the investigation. Which action fits?",["Isolate the device from the network","Offboard the device and re-image it straight away","Turn off tamper protection to allow cleanup","Restrict app execution to Microsoft-signed code"],3,"Restrict app execution stops unsigned code while the network stays up, so Microsoft-signed apps keep working. Isolation would cut the user off completely.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",["Isolation cuts all network traffic except to Defender, so the user could not keep using email and Teams.","Offboarding and re-imaging immediately destroys evidence and removes visibility before the investigation is done.","Turning off tamper protection weakens the device's defenses and does not stop the hacking tool.",null],null],["sa44",0,2,"A phishing email reached 300 mailboxes before it was detected. How should the analyst remove the copies?",["Ask each user to delete the email and empty their trash","Block the sender and wait for zero-hour auto purge","Find the messages in Threat Explorer and take a soft-delete action","Create a mail flow rule, which only affects messages sent in future"],2,"Threat Explorer (or advanced hunting) can find every delivered copy and remove them all at once, with the action tracked in the Action center. Blocking the sender affects only future mail.","Respond to security incidents: Investigate and respond to Microsoft 365 threats",["Relying on 300 users to delete the email is slow, unreliable and leaves copies behind.","Blocking the sender only affects future mail, and ZAP may not act on messages it does not reclassify.",null,"Mail flow rules act on messages in transit, so they cannot remove copies already delivered to mailboxes."],null],["sa45",0,2,"Where does an analyst review emails that users flagged with the built-in Report button?",["The User reported tab on the Submissions page","The Quarantine page under Review","The reports page for Attack simulation training","The Purview content search results"],0,"User-reported messages appear on the Submissions page, where analysts can triage them and send them to Microsoft. Quarantine holds messages the filters caught, not ones users reported.","Respond to security incidents: Investigate and respond to Microsoft 365 threats",[null,"Quarantine holds messages that filtering policies caught, not messages users reported with the Report button.","Attack simulation training reports cover simulated phishing campaigns, not real messages users reported.","Purview content search finds content for eDiscovery; it is not the triage queue for user-reported messages."],null],["sa46",0,2,"Defender for Identity raises 'Suspected DCSync attack' from a workstation. What does this alert mean?",["A workstation's clock is out of sync with the domain controller","A non-DC host asked a domain controller to replicate directory data, such as password hashes","A user changed their password on a domain-joined device","Group Policy failed to replicate between domain controllers"],1,"DCSync abuses replication rights to pull credential material. Only domain controllers should replicate, so a request from a workstation strongly suggests credential theft. It has nothing to do with time sync.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Identity",["DCSync has nothing to do with clock time; it abuses directory replication to steal credentials.",null,"A normal password change is routine and does not involve a workstation requesting replication from a DC.","Replication failures between DCs are an operational issue; DCSync is a non-DC impersonating a DC to pull secrets."],null],["sa47",0,2,"The analyst wants to see how compromising a help desk workstation could let an attacker reach a Domain Admin account through saved sessions and local admin rights. Which Defender for Identity feature shows this?",["Honeytoken alerts","Entity tags","Lateral movement paths","Sensor health"],2,"Lateral movement paths map how attackers could hop through machines and credentials to reach sensitive accounts. Honeytokens are decoy accounts; they don't map paths.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Identity",["Honeytoken alerts fire when decoy accounts are used; they do not map paths to sensitive accounts.","Entity tags mark accounts or devices as sensitive but do not show how an attacker could reach them.",null,"Sensor health reports on Defender for Identity sensor status, not attack paths."],null],["sa48",0,2,"During a human-operated ransomware attack, Defender XDR contained a compromised user account and several devices by itself within minutes. Which capability did this?",["A Sentinel automation rule on incident creation","A custom detection rule","Attack simulation training","Automatic attack disruption"],3,"Automatic attack disruption uses high-confidence XDR signals to contain compromised users and devices automatically. Custom detections raise alerts; they don't contain anything on their own.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",["Sentinel automation rules act on incidents you configure; they are not the built-in XDR capability that contained this attack.","Custom detection rules raise alerts from your queries and can take configured actions, but they did not provide this built-in, signal-driven containment.","Attack simulation training runs phishing simulations for users; it does not contain real attacks.",null],null],["sa49",0,2,"The SOC has confirmed a user's credentials were stolen and wants Entra ID Protection risk policies to treat the user as high risk. Which action should they take?",["Dismiss user risk","Add the user to a watchlist","Confirm user compromised","Disable UEBA for the user"],2,"'Confirm user compromised' sets the user's risk to high, which triggers risk-based Conditional Access and helps train the detections. Dismissing risk does the opposite.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to Microsoft Entra ID risks",["Dismissing user risk sets the risk to none, which is the opposite of what the SOC wants.","Watchlists are Sentinel reference data and have no effect on Entra ID Protection risk levels.",null,"Disabling UEBA removes behavioral insights in Sentinel and does not raise the user's Entra risk."],null],["sa50",0,2,"An investigation finds an attacker created an inbox rule that forwards a manager's mail to an outside address. Besides resetting the password, what should the responder do?",["Only turn on MFA, since the rule expires on its own","Leave the rule so the attacker isn't alerted","Delete the mailbox and restore it from backup","Remove the rule and revoke the user's sessions"],3,"Inbox rules persist after a password reset, and existing sessions can stay valid. Removing the rule and revoking refresh tokens both remove the attacker's access.","Respond to security incidents: Investigate and respond to Microsoft 365 threats",["Turning on MFA does not remove the rule, and inbox rules do not expire; mail would keep forwarding.","Leaving the rule in place keeps leaking the manager's mail to the attacker.","Deleting and restoring the mailbox is disruptive and unnecessary when the malicious rule can simply be removed.",null],null],["sa51",0,2,"A user reports dozens of MFA push prompts at night that they didn't start. What should the analyst do first?",["Tell the user to approve one prompt to stop the notifications","Reset the password and revoke sessions, since the password is likely known","Turn off MFA for the user until the prompts stop","Wait for Identity Protection to raise the risk level"],1,"Repeated prompts mean someone already has the password and is attempting MFA fatigue. Resetting and revoking stops them. Approving a prompt would give the attacker access.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to Microsoft Entra ID risks",["Approving a prompt would complete the attacker's sign-in and hand them access to the account.",null,"Turning off MFA removes the last barrier protecting an account whose password is already known.","Waiting leaves the account exposed while the attacker keeps trying; the user report is enough to act on."],null],["sa52",0,2,"Defender for Cloud Apps flags a newly consented OAuth app with mail read-write permissions that is sending unusual volumes of email. What is the best containment step?",["Ban or revoke the app so its consent and tokens stop working","Reset the passwords of users who consented","Add the app's publisher to a watchlist","Increase the anomaly policy threshold"],0,"OAuth apps use their own tokens, so a password reset doesn't remove their access. Banning or revoking the app removes it. Changing thresholds only hides alerts.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Cloud Apps",[null,"OAuth apps keep their own tokens and consent, so password resets do not remove the app's access.","A watchlist entry for the publisher only helps queries; it does not stop the app sending mail.","Raising the threshold hides the alert without stopping the malicious app."],null],["sa53",0,2,"The SOC wants Microsoft Purview DLP alerts triaged in the same place as endpoint and identity alerts. Where can analysts do this?",["The Entra admin center sign-in logs","The Azure Activity log","The Intune device compliance report","The Defender portal incident queue"],3,"Purview DLP alerts integrate with Defender XDR incidents, so they can be correlated with other signals in one queue. Sign-in logs don't contain DLP alerts.","Respond to security incidents: Investigate data loss prevention and insider risk alerts",["Entra sign-in logs show authentication events, not Purview DLP alerts.","The Azure Activity log records Azure resource management operations, not DLP policy matches.","Intune compliance reports show device compliance state, not DLP alerts or incidents.",null],null],["sa54",0,2,"In a Defender for Cloud security alert for a suspicious process on an Azure VM, which part lists mitigation steps and lets you trigger a Logic App or create a suppression rule?",["The Take action tab","The Recommendations blade","The Regulatory compliance dashboard","The Inventory blade"],0,"The alert's Take action tab has mitigation guidance, related recommendations, automated responses and suppression options. Recommendations cover posture, not a specific alert.","Respond to security incidents: Perform threat response with Microsoft Defender for Cloud",[null,"The Recommendations page covers posture findings across resources, not response actions for this specific alert.","The Regulatory compliance dashboard maps controls to standards; it has no per-alert mitigation or automation options.","Inventory lists resources and their coverage; it does not host alert response actions."],null],["sa55",0,2,"In Sentinel, the analyst wants to visually expand an incident's entities to find related alerts, hosts and accounts. Which tool fits?",["The data connector status page","The investigation graph","The workspace usage workbook","The analytics rule wizard"],1,"The investigation graph starts from an incident's entities and lets you expand related alerts and entities. A workbook shows prepared charts, not interactive entity pivoting.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to incidents in Microsoft Sentinel",["The data connector page shows connector status and configuration, not relationships between incident entities.",null,"The usage workbook shows ingestion volume charts, not an interactive graph of incident entities.","The analytics rule wizard is for creating or editing detection rules, not investigating incidents."],null],["sa56",0,2,"An analyst wants to know whether an account's activity is unusual compared with its normal pattern and its peers. Where in Sentinel should they look?",["The Content hub solution page","The MITRE ATT&CK coverage page","The account's entity page with UEBA insights","The SOC optimization page"],2,"Entity pages show timelines, related alerts and UEBA anomalies with peer comparisons. MITRE coverage shows rule coverage, not user behavior.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to incidents in Microsoft Sentinel",["Content hub solution pages describe installable content, not an account's behavior.","The MITRE ATT&CK page shows detection coverage by technique, not user behavior.",null,"SOC optimization recommends ingestion and coverage improvements; it has no per-account behavioral analysis."],null],["sa57",0,2,"An incident was caused by an approved penetration test. How should it be classified when resolved?",["False positive, because nothing malicious happened","True positive for malicious activity with no further notes","Expected activity (benign positive) with a security testing determination","Left active until the test report is published"],2,"The detection was correct but the activity was authorized, so it is an expected/benign-positive result. A false positive would suggest the rule is broken and push the team to tune a working detection.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",["False positive means the detection logic was wrong, but here the rule correctly detected real attack-like activity.","True positive for malicious activity misrepresents an authorized test and skews metrics.",null,"Leaving the incident active clutters the queue; it can be resolved now with the correct classification and notes."],null],["sa58",0,2,"An analyst new to a complex incident wants a plain-language summary and recommended next steps inside the incident page. What should they use?",["The Sentinel workspace usage workbook","Microsoft Security Copilot embedded in the Defender portal","A search job on the SecurityIncident table","The Action center history"],1,"Embedded Security Copilot creates incident summaries and guided response suggestions in the incident view. A search job retrieves archived data; it doesn't summarize anything.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",["The usage workbook shows ingestion metrics and has no incident summaries or guidance.",null,"A search job retrieves archived data into a table; it does not summarize an incident.","The Action center history lists remediation actions already taken, not a summary or next steps."],null],["sa59",0,2,"An alert contains a long, obfuscated PowerShell command. The analyst wants a quick explanation of what the script does. Which feature helps most?",["Security Copilot script analysis","Device discovery","Alert tuning rules for the PowerShell alert","Secure score"],0,"Copilot script analysis explains suspicious scripts and command lines in plain language. Alert tuning suppresses alerts; it doesn't explain them.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",[null,"Device discovery finds unmanaged devices on the network and says nothing about a script's behavior.","Alert tuning suppresses or resolves alerts; it does not explain the PowerShell.","Secure score measures posture; it cannot analyze a command line."],null],["sa60",0,2,"The analyst needs to see every process, file, registry and network event on a device in the minutes before and after an alert. Where should they look?",["The device's vulnerability list","The Secure score history","The Submissions page","The device timeline"],3,"The device timeline shows the device's events in time order, with filtering around the alert time. Vulnerability lists show missing patches, not activity.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",["The vulnerability list shows missing patches and CVEs, not process, file or network activity.","Secure score history shows posture changes over time, not device events.","The Submissions page is for sending emails, files and URLs to Microsoft for analysis, not for viewing device activity.",null],null],["sa61",0,2,"An automated investigation is waiting for approval to quarantine a file. Where does the analyst approve it?",["The Advanced features page in endpoint settings","The Pending tab of the Action center","The Threat analytics report","The Secure score page"],1,"The Action center lists remediation actions awaiting approval (Pending) and completed actions (History). Advanced features settings turn capabilities on or off; they don't approve individual actions.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",["Advanced features settings turn capabilities on or off for the tenant; they do not list actions awaiting approval.",null,"Threat analytics reports describe threat campaigns and exposure; they have no approval queue.","Secure score tracks posture improvements and cannot approve remediation actions."],null],["sa62",0,2,"Defender for Identity reports suspected Golden Ticket usage. Which remediation invalidates forged Kerberos tickets?",["Reset the passwords of all users who logged on that day","Restart all domain controllers at the same time","Turn off Kerberos and use NTLM temporarily","Reset the KRBTGT password twice, waiting for replication between resets"],3,"Golden Tickets are signed with the KRBTGT hash. Resetting it twice (because the previous password is also accepted) invalidates the forged tickets. Resetting user passwords doesn't change the key that signs them.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Identity",["User password resets do not change the KRBTGT key that signs forged TGTs, so the Golden Tickets stay valid.","Restarting domain controllers does not change the KRBTGT key and would cause an outage for nothing.","Disabling Kerberos is not practical and does not invalidate the stolen KRBTGT secret.",null],null],["sa63",0,2,"Every analyst should follow the same containment checklist inside each ransomware incident in Sentinel. What provides this?",["Incident tasks added by an automation rule","A watchlist named 'checklist'","A bookmark on each incident","A workbook pinned to the SOC's shared dashboard"],0,"Automation rules can add incident tasks so every analyst sees and ticks off the same steps inside the incident. A workbook sits outside the incident and can't track completion.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to incidents in Microsoft Sentinel",[null,"A watchlist named checklist is only queryable reference data; it does not appear as trackable steps inside incidents.","Bookmarks save hunting query results as evidence; they are not a checklist analysts work through.","A shared workbook sits outside the incident and cannot track which steps were completed for each one."],null],["sa64",0,2,"An analyst finds two Defender XDR incidents that are the same attack on the same user. What should they do?",["Keep both open so each gets separate metrics","Delete one incident from the queue","Link the alerts into one incident and resolve the other","Create a suppression rule for the second incident's alerts"],2,"Moving (linking) alerts into one incident keeps all the evidence together; the empty incident can then be resolved. Suppression rules would hide future real alerts.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in the Defender portal",["Keeping duplicates splits evidence across two incidents and inflates metrics.","Incidents are not deleted from the queue; the right move is to consolidate the alerts and resolve the empty incident.",null,"Suppressing the second incident's alerts would hide future genuine alerts matching the same pattern."],null],["sa65",0,2,"After confirming a ransomware binary's SHA-256 hash, the SOC wants it blocked from running on every onboarded device. What should they create?",["An alert tuning rule for the hash","A Sentinel watchlist containing the hash","A device group for affected devices","A file hash indicator with the Block and remediate action"],3,"File indicators with Block and remediate prevent the file from running and remediate it wherever it is found. A watchlist can be used in queries but blocks nothing.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",["Alert tuning suppresses alerts about the file but does not block it from running.","A watchlist can be referenced in queries but enforces nothing on endpoints.","A device group scopes permissions and automation; it does not block a file hash.",null],null],["sa66",0,2,"An impossible-travel alert shows sign-ins from London and Singapore ten minutes apart. The Singapore IP is the company's VPN egress. What is the best response?",["Confirm it's corporate egress, close as benign and tag the range as corporate","Disable the account and reset the password at once","Delete the anomaly detection policy","Add Singapore to a blocked countries list"],0,"Verifying the IP shows expected activity; tagging corporate ranges reduces future false alerts. Deleting the policy removes a useful detection.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Cloud Apps",[null,"Disabling the account and resetting the password disrupts a legitimate user when the IP is known corporate VPN egress.","Deleting the anomaly policy removes a useful detection for everyone instead of tuning out known corporate IPs.","Blocking Singapore would block the company's own VPN egress and does not address the benign alert."],null],["sa67",0,2,"The analyst needs a list of SharePoint files a compromised account downloaded last week. Which data source gives this?",["Entra ID sign-in logs","Microsoft Purview Audit (the unified audit log)","The Defender for Endpoint timeline of the user's device","Azure Activity log"],1,"The unified audit log records SharePoint and OneDrive file actions such as FileDownloaded. Sign-in logs show authentication, not file access.","Respond to security incidents: Investigate and respond to Microsoft 365 threats",["Sign-in logs record authentication to apps, not which SharePoint files were downloaded.",null,"The device timeline covers activity on that one device and misses downloads from other devices or browsers.","The Azure Activity log records Azure resource management operations, not SharePoint file access."],null],["sa68",0,2,"An analyst wants to run an enrichment playbook on demand against one Sentinel incident. How can they do this?",["Edit the analytics rule and rerun it","Create a new data connector for the playbook","Run the incident-triggered playbook from the incident's actions","Restore the incident's logs from the data lake"],2,"Playbooks with the incident trigger can be run manually from the incident page. Rerunning the rule doesn't trigger enrichment.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to incidents in Microsoft Sentinel",["Rerunning the analytics rule creates new alerts at best and does not run the enrichment playbook.","Playbooks are not data connectors; creating a connector does nothing to run a Logic App.",null,"Restoring logs brings archived data back for queries; it does not trigger a playbook."],null],["sa69",0,2,"Defender for Cloud Apps shows a user mass-downloading files from SharePoint at 3 a.m. The analyst wants to block the account from signing in while the investigation continues. Which governance action fits?",["Dismiss the alert and watch for another one","Create a new file policy for future downloads","Suspend the user","Add the user to a Sentinel watchlist of VIP accounts"],2,"Suspending the user blocks sign-in (through Entra ID for Microsoft 365) straight away, which stops the activity. A new file policy only affects future matching files and does not stop this user.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Cloud Apps",["Dismissing the alert leaves the account active while it may be exfiltrating data.","A new file policy applies to matching files going forward and does not stop this user from signing in.",null,"A VIP watchlist entry changes how queries treat the user but does not block sign-in."],null],["sa70",0,2,"A device was isolated during an incident, then cleaned and confirmed safe. What should the analyst do so the user can work normally again?",["Release the device from isolation in the device page","Offboard the device and onboard it again","Run the investigation package collection once more","Turn off network protection on that device"],0,"Release from isolation restores normal network connectivity and is recorded in the Action center. Offboarding and re-onboarding is unnecessary and leaves a gap in monitoring.","Respond to security incidents: Respond to alerts and incidents in Defender for Endpoint",[null,"Offboarding and re-onboarding is unnecessary and creates a gap in monitoring.","Collecting another investigation package gathers data but leaves the device isolated.","Turning off network protection weakens the device and has nothing to do with lifting isolation."],null],["sa71",0,3,"Which KQL filter returns only events from the last 24 hours in a Defender advanced hunting table?",["| where Timestamp < ago(1d)","| where Timestamp > ago(1d)","| take 1d","| summarize by ago(1d)"],1,"ago(1d) is the time 24 hours ago, so Timestamp greater than it means newer events. Using < returns everything older than a day.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["Timestamp < ago(1d) returns events older than 24 hours, the opposite of what is needed.",null,"take limits the number of rows returned and does not accept a time span such as 1d.","summarize by ago(1d) groups by a constant value and does not filter to the last day."],null],["sa72",0,3,"A hunter searches ProcessCommandLine for the whole word 'mimikatz' across billions of rows. Which operator is usually fastest?",["contains","matches regex","startswith_cs","has"],3,"has uses the term index to match whole terms, so it is much faster than contains, which scans for substrings. Use contains only for partial matches inside a word.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["contains scans for substrings and cannot use the term index, so it is slower on large tables.","Regular expressions are the most expensive string matching and should be a last resort.","startswith_cs only matches the beginning of the value, so it would miss mimikatz appearing later in the command line.",null],null],["sa73",0,3,"Which query counts failed sign-ins per user?",["SigninLogs | project count() by UserPrincipalName | where ResultType != \"0\"","SigninLogs | extend count() | where ResultType == \"0\"","SigninLogs | where ResultType != \"0\" | take UserPrincipalName","SigninLogs | where ResultType != \"0\" | summarize count() by UserPrincipalName"],3,"summarize count() by groups rows and counts each group; filtering first on non-zero ResultType keeps only failures. project and take don't aggregate.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["project does not aggregate, and count() by is not valid inside project; the filter is also applied after the attempted count.","extend cannot take count() as an aggregation, and ResultType == \"0\" selects successful sign-ins, not failures.","take returns a number of rows and does not accept a column name or produce counts per user.",null],null],["sa74",0,3,"A hunter wants logon counts in one-hour buckets to spot spikes. Which expression belongs in the summarize?",["count() by bin(Timestamp, 1h)","count() by Timestamp","count() by ago(1h)","count() by format_datetime(1h)"],0,"bin() rounds timestamps down into fixed-size buckets, so each hour becomes one row. Grouping by the raw Timestamp gives one row per unique timestamp.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",[null,"Grouping by the raw Timestamp gives one row per unique time value, not hourly buckets.","ago(1h) returns a single point in time, so grouping by it would not create hourly buckets.","format_datetime formats a datetime value as text and does not take 1h as its argument; it does not create buckets."],null],["sa75",0,3,"A hunter wants to add process details from DeviceProcessEvents to network connections in DeviceNetworkEvents, matching on DeviceId and process ID. Which operator does this?",["union","join","extend","distinct"],1,"join combines columns from two tables on matching keys. union stacks rows from tables without matching them.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["union stacks rows from multiple tables without matching them on keys, so columns would not be combined.",null,"extend adds calculated columns from the same row; it cannot bring in columns from another table.","distinct removes duplicate rows from a single result set and does not combine tables."],null],["sa76",0,3,"A hunter has 20 known-bad IP addresses from a threat report and wants to reuse them in several parts of a query. What is the cleanest approach?",["Repeat twenty separate or conditions in each where clause of the query","Use project badIPs at the start of the query","let badIPs = dynamic([...]); then where RemoteIP in (badIPs)","Put the list in a comment above the query"],2,"let defines a named value once; in() checks membership against the dynamic array. Repeating conditions works but is error-prone and hard to maintain.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["Twenty repeated or conditions work but are error-prone and must be edited everywhere when the list changes.","project selects columns from a table; it cannot define a reusable list of values.",null,"A comment is ignored by the query engine, so the IPs would not be used for filtering."],null],["sa77",0,3,"What is the difference between project and extend in KQL?",["project adds rows; extend adds tables","project picks the output columns; extend adds new columns to the existing ones","project sorts results; extend filters them","They are identical aliases for the same operator"],1,"extend keeps all existing columns and adds calculated ones, while project returns only the columns you list (and can calculate them too).","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["Neither operator adds rows or tables; both work on the columns of the current result.",null,"Sorting is done with order by or sort by, and filtering with where, not project or extend.","They behave differently: project drops columns you do not list, while extend keeps them all."],null],["sa78",0,3,"Which advanced hunting table holds delivery details such as sender, recipient, subject and delivery action for each email?",["EmailUrlInfo","DeviceEvents","IdentityLogonEvents","EmailEvents"],3,"EmailEvents has one row per email with sender, recipient, subject and delivery details. EmailUrlInfo lists only the URLs found in emails.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["EmailUrlInfo lists URLs found in emails, not the sender, recipient, subject and delivery action.","DeviceEvents holds miscellaneous endpoint events such as ASR and exploit protection, not email data.","IdentityLogonEvents records authentication activity from identity sources, not email delivery.",null],null],["sa79",0,3,"A hunter looks for encoded PowerShell launched by Office apps on endpoints. Which table should the query start from?",["DeviceProcessEvents","DeviceNetworkEvents","EmailAttachmentInfo","CloudAppEvents"],0,"DeviceProcessEvents records process creation with command lines and parent processes (InitiatingProcessFileName). Network events have connections but not full process-creation detail.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",[null,"DeviceNetworkEvents records network connections, not process creation with full command lines.","EmailAttachmentInfo describes email attachments, not processes running on endpoints.","CloudAppEvents holds activity in cloud apps and services, not endpoint process launches."],null],["sa80",0,3,"Which advanced hunting table has on-premises Active Directory authentication events seen by Defender for Identity?",["DeviceLogonEvents","AADSignInEventsBeta","IdentityLogonEvents","EmailEvents"],2,"IdentityLogonEvents records authentication seen by Defender for Identity on domain controllers (and some cloud logons). DeviceLogonEvents comes from endpoints, not DC traffic.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["DeviceLogonEvents comes from Defender for Endpoint on devices, not authentication traffic seen on domain controllers.","AADSignInEventsBeta holds Entra ID (cloud) sign-ins, not on-premises Active Directory authentication.",null,"EmailEvents contains email delivery details and has nothing to do with AD authentication."],null],["sa81",0,3,"During a Sentinel hunt, an analyst finds suspicious rows and wants to save them with notes and entities so they can later be added to an incident. What should they create?",["A watchlist","A workbook","A summary rule","A bookmark"],3,"Bookmarks keep query results with notes, tags and entity mappings, and can be attached to or used to create incidents. A watchlist holds reference data, not evidence.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",["A watchlist holds reference data such as VIP lists, not evidence with notes from a hunt.","A workbook visualizes query results but does not save specific rows with notes and entities for incidents.","A summary rule writes scheduled aggregations to a table; it does not save specific findings as evidence.",null],null],["sa82",0,3,"A hunter wants to be notified as soon as new events match a hunting query during an active investigation, without creating an analytics rule. Which feature fits?",["Livestream","A search job","A restore operation","Content hub"],0,"Livestream runs a hunting query continuously against new data and notifies you of matches. Search jobs look back at stored data rather than watching new events.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",[null,"A search job scans stored long-term data asynchronously; it does not watch new events as they arrive.","Restore brings archived data back for querying and does not notify you of new matches.","Content hub installs solutions and content; it does not run queries against new data."],null],["sa83",0,3,"A new IOC must be checked against firewall logs from 18 months ago that sit only in long-term retention. Which feature is designed for this?",["Livestream","A search job","An NRT analytics rule","A workbook"],1,"Search jobs scan long-term data asynchronously and write matches to a new table you can query. Livestream and NRT rules only look at newly arriving data.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",["Livestream only watches newly arriving data, so it cannot see logs from 18 months ago.",null,"NRT rules run every minute on newly ingested data and cannot scan long-term retention.","Workbooks run interactive queries on the analytics tier and are not designed to scan long-term retention."],null],["sa84",0,3,"A hunter wants a daily KQL query over data lake data whose results are written to an analytics-tier table for detections to use. What should they create?",["A livestream session on the data lake tables","An automation rule","A KQL job in the Sentinel data lake","A watchlist"],2,"Data lake KQL jobs run queries (once or on a schedule) against the lake and can promote results to the analytics tier. Automation rules act on incidents, not on data.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",["Livestream watches new analytics-tier data interactively; it does not run scheduled jobs on data lake tables or write results to a table.","Automation rules act on incidents and alerts, not on scheduled queries over data.",null,"A watchlist is static reference data and cannot run a daily query."],null],["sa85",0,3,"The team wants to use Python, MSTICPy and machine learning to analyze Sentinel data beyond what KQL alone can do. What should they use?",["Workbooks with KQL visualizations","Watchlists","Sentinel notebooks (Jupyter)","Automation rules"],2,"Notebooks run Jupyter with libraries such as MSTICPy for enrichment, visualization and ML over Sentinel data. Workbooks only show KQL results.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",["Workbooks visualize KQL results but cannot run Python, MSTICPy or machine learning code.","Watchlists are reference data lists and run no analysis at all.",null,"Automation rules handle incidents (assign, tag, run playbooks), not data science analysis."],null],["sa86",0,3,"The SOC wants analyst reports on an active ransomware campaign, with which of their own devices are exposed and which mitigations are missing. Where is this?",["Threat analytics in Defender XDR","The MITRE ATT&CK coverage page in Sentinel","The Content hub","The Action center"],0,"Threat analytics reports combine Microsoft threat research with your tenant's exposure, alerts and mitigation status. MITRE coverage shows only which techniques your rules cover.","Perform threat hunting: Analyze threat intelligence",[null,"The MITRE ATT&CK page shows which techniques your rules cover, not campaign reports with your exposure and missing mitigations.","Content hub installs solutions and detections; it does not publish campaign analysis tied to your devices.","The Action center lists remediation actions, not threat reports or exposure details."],null],["sa87",0,3,"A hunter wants one query that finds connections to a malicious IP across logs from three firewall vendors without writing three separate queries. What should they use?",["A union of three raw tables with different column names","A separate workspace for each vendor","Three near-real-time rules, one per vendor","ASIM parsers such as _Im_NetworkSession"],3,"ASIM normalizes different sources into shared schemas, so one parser query covers every vendor. A raw union still needs field mapping for each vendor.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",["A raw union still needs vendor-specific field names for each table, so the query is effectively three queries glued together.","Separate workspaces make cross-vendor querying harder, not easier.","Three NRT rules are three separate detections, not one hunting query across vendors.",null],null],["sa88",0,3,"An analyst who is new to KQL wants to describe a hunt in plain English and get a starting advanced hunting query. What can help?",["The Action center query builder","Security Copilot generating KQL in advanced hunting","The Secure score query tool","A Defender for Cloud recommendation"],1,"Security Copilot in advanced hunting can turn natural-language requests into KQL, which the analyst should review before running. The other options don't write queries.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["The Action center lists remediation actions and has no query builder.",null,"Secure score measures posture and has no tool that writes hunting queries.","Defender for Cloud recommendations are posture fixes, not KQL queries for hunting."],null],["sa89",0,3,"A hunter wants to see what a compromised service account could reach (its blast radius) by following relationships between identities, devices and resources. Which capability fits?",["A take 100 query on the SigninLogs table for the account","A hunting graph built on the Sentinel graph","A watchlist of service accounts","An alert tuning rule for the account"],1,"Graph-based hunting models relationships between entities, so you can explore paths and blast radius from a starting entity. A flat query of sign-ins doesn't show what the account can reach.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using Microsoft Sentinel",["A flat query of the account's sign-ins shows where it logged in, not what resources its relationships let it reach.",null,"A watchlist of service accounts is only a reference list and shows no relationships or reach.","An alert tuning rule suppresses alerts and says nothing about what the account can access."],null],["sa90",0,3,"The AdditionalFields column holds JSON, and the hunter needs one property from it as a column. Which KQL approach works?",["project AdditionalFields.PropName as text()","where AdditionalFields has_json PropName","extend Prop = tostring(parse_json(AdditionalFields).PropName)","summarize by json(AdditionalFields)"],2,"parse_json turns the string into a dynamic object, you read the property with dot notation, and tostring makes it a usable string column. The other forms are not valid KQL.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["This is not valid KQL: dynamic properties need parse_json first, and there is no text() function used this way.","has_json is not a KQL operator, and a where clause filters rows instead of extracting a property as a column.",null,"json() is not a KQL function, and summarize by would group rows rather than extract one property."],null],["sa91",0,3,"A hunter wants to flag days when a user's outbound data volume is statistically unusual compared with that user's own history. Which KQL approach fits?",["make-series then series_decompose_anomalies()","distinct then take 10","sort by bytes then take the top 10 rows for each user","union then project-away"],0,"make-series builds a time series for each entity and series_decompose_anomalies scores outliers against the trend and seasonality. Sorting or taking the top values ignores each user's baseline.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",[null,"distinct and take just list a few unique values and do not compare anything with a user's history.","Sorting by bytes and taking top rows finds the largest values overall, not days that are unusual for that user's own baseline.","union combines tables and project-away drops columns; neither detects statistical anomalies."],null],["sa92",0,3,"A hunting query has found real attacker activity twice. What should the team do so they are alerted automatically next time?",["Bookmark the results and rerun the same query by hand when needed","Add the query text to a watchlist","Export the results to a CSV file each week","Save the query as a custom detection or analytics rule"],3,"Turning a proven hunting query into a detection rule runs it on a schedule and raises alerts. Bookmarks save past findings but won't catch new activity.","Perform threat hunting: Hunt for threats by using KQL",["Bookmarks preserve past findings, and rerunning a query by hand depends on someone remembering to do it.","Watchlists store reference data; placing query text there never runs it.","Weekly CSV exports are manual reports of past results and raise no alerts on new activity.",null],null]]);
